sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch08-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because turbulent randomness is generated intrinsically by large-scale motion, carefully prepared experiments should produce fluid flows that look random yet are essentially the same on every repeated run.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Random-looking yet repeatable fluid flows should be producible";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 8, “Implications for Everyday Systems”; section “Fluid Flow” (from p. 376), p. 382. Scope: laboratory fluid flows at turbulent or near-turbulent conditions"
}
